<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
    <PackageId>Excalibur.Dispatch.Aws</PackageId>
    <Description>Experience metapackage bundling Excalibur.Dispatch with AWS SQS transport. Provides a single AddDispatchAws() call for the common AWS messaging scenario.</Description>
    <PackageTags>excalibur;dispatch;aws;sqs;metapackage</PackageTags>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <PropertyGroup>
    <IsAotCompatible>true</IsAotCompatible>
    <EnableTrimAnalyzer>true</EnableTrimAnalyzer>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection.Abstractions" />
    <!--
      DIRECT ON PURPOSE, AND NOT USED BY ANY CODE HERE. Do not remove it as unreferenced.

      System.Formats.Cbor arrives transitively through the AWS SDK beneath the AwsSqs transport, and
      9.0.5 carries a moderate-severity advisory that NU1902 treats as an error in this repository. The
      central transitive pin that raises it everywhere else cannot raise it HERE, because metapackages
      deliberately opt out of central transitive pinning (see the reasoning in ../Directory.Build.props:
      pinning promotes the union of every referenced project's transitive graph onto the nuspec as direct
      dependencies, which once gave a package named for Postgres 60 direct dependencies including a PDF
      library and the MongoDB driver).

      So the floor is recorded and not applied: this project's lock file carried a CentralTransitive entry
      requesting [10.0.15, ) and resolving 9.0.5 — below its own floor — and deleting the lock file
      entirely reproduced it, so it is neither staleness nor a resolver fault. A direct reference is the
      first-party remedy for an advisory on a transitive dependency, and it is the mechanism that works
      here: the same floor as a Direct entry resolves 10.0.15.

      This adds exactly ONE dependency to the bundle's nuspec rather than re-enabling pinning, so the
      opt-out above and the dependency list it protects both stand. The version stays centrally managed in
      Directory.Packages.props; nothing is pinned twice.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="System.Formats.Cbor" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Dispatch\Excalibur.Dispatch\Excalibur.Dispatch.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Dispatch\Excalibur.Dispatch.Transport.AwsSqs\Excalibur.Dispatch.Transport.AwsSqs.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Dispatch\Excalibur.Dispatch.Resilience.Polly\Excalibur.Dispatch.Resilience.Polly.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\Dispatch\Excalibur.Dispatch.Observability\Excalibur.Dispatch.Observability.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
